      "dom_id": "question-101844",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve for $u$ where $\\frac{ut}{3}-t=2q.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to isolate $u,$ so we will treat the other variables $t$ and $q$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $u.$\n\nFirst, we add $t$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ut}{3}-t & =2q \\\\ \\frac{ut}{3}-t+t & =2q+t \\\\ \\frac{ut}{3} & =2q+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ut}{3} & =2q+t \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{ut}{3} & =3\\cdot (2q+t) \\\\ ut & =3(2q+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide by $t$ to get $u$ alone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ut & =3(2q+t) \\\\ \\frac{ut}{t} & =\\frac{3(2q+t)}{t} \\\\ u & =\\frac{3(2q+t)}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $t\\ne 0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
